  "content": {
    "id": "p21-q17-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "17",
    "prompt": "[Medley of algebraic techniques]\n\nSimplify the following, if possible.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 3,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p21-q17-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$y\\times y$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y^2$",
          "worked": "$y\\times y=y^2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 10
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-q17-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$y+y^2$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y+y^2$",
          "worked": "$y$ and $y^2$ are unlike terms, so the sum stays $y+y^2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 10
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-q17-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$y\\times y^2$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y^3$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}y\\times y^2&=y^{1+2}\\\\y^{1+2}&=y^3\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 10
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-q17-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$(2y)^2$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$4y^2$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}(2y)^2&=2^2y^2\\\\2^2y^2&=4y^2\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 10
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-q17-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "$2(x^2)^3$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2x^6$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}2(x^2)^3&=2x^{2\\cdot 3}\\\\2x^{2\\cdot 3}&=2x^6\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 10
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-q17-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "$(2x^2)^3$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$8x^6$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}(2x^2)^3&=2^3x^{2\\cdot 3}\\\\2^3x^{2\\cdot 3}&=8x^6\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 10
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-q17-g",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "g",
        "prompt": "$a^m\\times a^n$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$a^{m+n}$",
          "worked": "Add the indices when multiplying powers with the same base. $a^m\\times a^n=a^{m+n}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 10
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-q17-h",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "h",
        "prompt": "$(3a)^0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$1$ for $a\\ne 0$.",
          "worked": "A nonzero base to the power zero equals $1$, so $(3a)^0=1$ for $a\\ne 0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 10
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-q17-i",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "i",
        "prompt": "$\\frac{x+y}{x}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$1+\\frac{y}{x}$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{x+y}{x}&=\\frac{x}{x}+\\frac{y}{x}\\\\\\frac{x}{x}+\\frac{y}{x}&=1+\\frac{y}{x}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-q17-j",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "j",
        "prompt": "$\\frac{x+y}{xy}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{x}$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{x+y}{xy}&=\\frac{x}{xy}+\\frac{y}{xy}\\\\\\frac{x}{xy}+\\frac{y}{xy}&=\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{x}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-q17-k",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "k",
        "prompt": "$\\frac{x+xy}{x}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$1+y$ for $x\\ne 0$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{x+xy}{x}&=\\frac{x(1+y)}{x}\\\\\\frac{x(1+y)}{x}&=1+y\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe original denominator requires $x\\ne 0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 10
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-q17-l",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "l",
        "prompt": "$\\frac{1}{2}(4x+6y)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2x+3y$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\tfrac12(4x+6y)&=\\tfrac12(4x)+\\tfrac12(6y)\\\\\\tfrac12(4x)+\\tfrac12(6y)&=2x+3y\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 10
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-q17-m",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "m",
        "prompt": "$\\frac{4x+6y}{2x}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2+\\frac{3y}{x}$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{4x+6y}{2x}&=\\frac{2(2x+3y)}{2x}\\\\\\frac{2(2x+3y)}{2x}&=\\frac{2x+3y}{x}\\\\\\frac{2x+3y}{x}&=2+\\frac{3y}{x}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 17
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-q17-n",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "n",
        "prompt": "$\\frac{4(x+y)}{4x}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$1+\\frac{y}{x}$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{4(x+y)}{4x}&=\\frac{x+y}{x}\\\\\\frac{x+y}{x}&=\\frac{x}{x}+\\frac{y}{x}\\\\\\frac{x}{x}+\\frac{y}{x}&=1+\\frac{y}{x}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 14
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-q17-o",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "o",
        "prompt": "$\\frac{6xy}{3+6y}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{2xy}{1+2y}$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{6xy}{3+6y}&=\\frac{3(2xy)}{3(1+2y)}\\\\\\frac{3(2xy)}{3(1+2y)}&=\\frac{2xy}{1+2y}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 14
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-q17-p",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "p",
        "prompt": "$\\frac{4x+x}{4}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{5x}{4}$",
          "worked": "Combine the like terms in the numerator. $\\frac{4x+x}{4}=\\frac{5x}{4}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-q17-q",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "q",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac{\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{y}}{xy}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{x+y}{x^2y^2}$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{y}&=\\frac{x+y}{xy}\\\\\\frac{\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{y}}{xy}&=\\frac{x+y}{xy}\\cdot\\frac{1}{xy}\\\\\\frac{x+y}{xy}\\cdot\\frac{1}{xy}&=\\frac{x+y}{x^2y^2}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 21
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-q17-r",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "r",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac{\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{y}}{\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{y}}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{y+x}{y-x}$, with $x\\ne 0$, $y\\ne 0$ and $x\\ne y$.\n\nMultiply the numerator and denominator of the complex fraction by $xy$.",
          "worked": "Multiply the numerator and denominator by $xy$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}xy\\left(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{y}\\right)&=y+x\\\\xy\\left(\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{y}\\right)&=y-x\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe simplified fraction is $\\frac{y+x}{y-x}$. The original expression requires $x\\ne 0$, $y\\ne 0$ and $x\\ne y$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 27
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-q17-s",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "s",
        "prompt": "$\\frac{2}{x+y}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{2}{x+y}$",
          "worked": "The numerator and denominator have no common factor to cancel. The fraction stays $\\frac{2}{x+y}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-q17-t",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "t",
        "prompt": "$\\frac{x-y}{y-x}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$-1$ for $x\\ne y$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{x-y}{y-x}&=\\frac{x-y}{-(x-y)}\\\\\\frac{x-y}{-(x-y)}&=-1\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe original denominator requires $x\\ne y$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 10
      }
    ]
